 "location": {
  "name": "Petra",
  "wiki": "Petra",
  "country": "Jordan",
  "region": "Ma'an Governorate, southern Jordan",
  "lat": 30.3285,
  "lon": 35.4444,
  "tagline": "A whole city carved into rose-red cliffs.",
  "intro": "Petra was the capital of the Nabataeans, desert traders who got rich controlling the incense routes between Arabia and the Mediterranean. Instead of building with stone, they carved temples, tombs and a theater straight into the sandstone canyon walls.",
  "stats": [
   {
    "label": "Country",
    "value": "Jordan"
   },
   {
    "label": "Built by",
    "value": "Nabataeans"
   },
   {
    "label": "UNESCO site since",
    "value": "1985"
   },
   {
    "label": "New 7 Wonders",
    "value": "Named 2007"
   }
  ],
  "facts": [
   "You reach the city through the Siq, a narrow gorge about 1.2 km long with walls that tower overhead.",
   "The famous Treasury (Al-Khazneh) is a tomb, not a treasury. The name comes from a legend that pirates hid gold in the urn on top, and you can still see the bullet marks from people shooting at it.",
   "The West lost track of Petra for centuries until the Swiss explorer Johann Ludwig Burckhardt reached it in disguise in 1812.",
   "The Nabataeans engineered dams, cisterns and channels to collect flash-flood water, which let a large city survive in the desert.",
   "The Treasury's facade is the temple at the end of Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade (1989)."
  ],
  "bestTime": "March to May or September to November, when it's warm but not scorching.",
  "gettingThere": "Fly JFK to Amman (Royal Jordanian flies nonstop), then about a 3-hour drive south.",
  "tip": "Hike the 800-plus steps up to the Monastery (Ad Deir). It's bigger than the Treasury and far less crowded."
 },
